module bindings.javascript.fail_open_policy_declaration_guard;

// An authorization dispatcher consults the permission a resource requires
// through a membership test over the collection the caller was actually
// granted -- `!token.permission.some(p => p === ep.meta.kind)`,
// `!user.scopes.includes(route.scope)`, `!client.claims.has(scope.claim)` --
// and conjoins that test with the bare presence of the requirement itself:
// `if (token && ep.meta.kind && !token.permission.some(...))`. The conjunction
// is the defect. Wherever the requirement was never declared the guard is
// false, so the check it guards is skipped rather than failed, and a caller
// whose grant covers nothing reaches the operation. The remediation names the
// undeclared case instead: `... || (!ep.meta.kind && deny)`, or
// `ep.meta.kind == null` in that branch, and either spelling clears the
// report.
//
// Both facts are structural and produced by the JavaScript frontend for this
// purpose. `fail_open_policy_declaration_guard` is the relation between the
// sibling conjuncts -- one is the bare read X of a declared field, another is
// a negated membership call comparing against X, and no operand of the same
// condition tests X's absence -- so nothing here names what any application
// calls its requirement field. The collection half is the documented
// permission-collection vocabulary -- OAuth's `scope`/`scopes`, RBAC `roles`,
// Spring Security's `authorities`, OIDC's `claims`, and the `permissions`,
// `grants`, `privileges`, `entitlements`, `capabilities` and `accessRights`
// that authorization frameworks and token libraries use for the granted set --
// crossed with the membership calls that answer "is this element in that
// collection". Requiring the noun on the membership call itself, rather than
// anywhere in the function, keeps a function that merely reads a permissions
// object elsewhere from pairing with an unrelated guarded allowlist.
//
// Residual, stated: a granted collection named outside that vocabulary (an
// application that calls it `token.flags`) is invisible, which is the
// false-negative direction; a requirement held in a plain identifier rather
// than a field (`if (scope && !token.scopes.includes(scope))`), or spelled
// only inside a presence test (`ep.meta.kind != null && ...`), does not carry
// the fact, since the fact keys on a bare declared-field conjunct; the
// membership test inside the guard is not itself verified to deny, only to
// be skipped when the requirement is absent; and the pairing is with a
// permission-collection membership call anywhere in the same function, not
// necessarily the guard's own membership call, so a fail-open-shaped guard
// over an unrelated collection in a function that also calls
// `user.permissions.includes(...)` elsewhere reports. Across the clone
// corpus no other file carrying the shape contains a permission-collection
// membership call at all, so that direction is unobserved in practice.
binding failOpenPolicyDeclarationGuard {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.failOpenPolicyDeclarationGuard == "true"
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, [
      ".permission.some", ".permission.includes", ".permission.has", ".permission.contains",
      ".permissions.some", ".permissions.includes", ".permissions.has", ".permissions.contains",
      ".scope.some", ".scope.includes", ".scope.has", ".scope.contains",
      ".scopes.some", ".scopes.includes", ".scopes.has", ".scopes.contains",
      ".roles.some", ".roles.includes", ".roles.has", ".roles.contains",
      ".authorities.some", ".authorities.includes", ".authorities.has", ".authorities.contains",
      ".grants.some", ".grants.includes", ".grants.has", ".grants.contains",
      ".privileges.some", ".privileges.includes", ".privileges.has", ".privileges.contains",
      ".claims.some", ".claims.includes", ".claims.has", ".claims.contains",
      ".entitlements.some", ".entitlements.includes", ".entitlements.has", ".entitlements.contains",
      ".capabilities.some", ".capabilities.includes", ".capabilities.has", ".capabilities.contains",
      ".accessRights.some", ".accessRights.includes", ".accessRights.has", ".accessRights.contains"
    ])
  emit issue code.UndeclaredScopeSkipsEnforcement at node
}
